  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nUpper level ridging has begun to break down due to a shortwave\ntraversing across the Midwest as of 9 PM. Little to no change will\nbe felt across the Mid-South until this weekend. Until Saturday,\nexpect dry conditions with high temperatures generally in the 70s\nto low 80s. A slow change in humidity will be felt as return flow\nstrengthens and advects moisture into the region. With this, RH \nvalues will slowly climb above critical thresholds by Thursday at \nthe latest. Continue to heed all local burn bans as drought \nconditions encompass the region and continue to cause dry fuels. \n\nThis weekend\u2019s pattern shift is due to an upper level low\ndeveloping over the Central Plains. This will bring heightened\nrain chances for Halloween, with the latest guidance trending\nlater with the bulk of the precipitation starting after 7 PM\nHalloween night. The deterministic GFS remains an outlier with an\nearlier onset. LREF soundings do not show the column being\nsaturated until later in the afternoon and evening, so this is\nsupportive of the latest trends. Due to the dry air and timing of\nthe system, there is a 30-40% chance of accumulation exceeding one\ninch along the Tennessee River Valley with lesser amounts\nelsewhere. \n\nOnce this feature swings through, a brief lull will return to\nstart the work week. Early next week, most guidance wants to bring\nanother system towards the Mid-South, but disagreements on\nevolution and timing keep details limited. The GFS seems to be the\noutlier once again at this point, with a very tight pressure\ngradient; albeit the LREF has severe weather ingredients (>500\nJ/kg of CAPE and >30kts of shear) less than 25%. Nonetheless, this\nis still in the long range and we will monitor the evolution of\nthis system.\n\nDNM",
